/* AIOPEDIA — catalog: models (landmark systems and model families) */
window.AIOPEDIA = window.AIOPEDIA || {};
window.AIOPEDIA.models = [
  { id: "perceptron", name: "Perceptron", category: "models", year: 1958, tagline: "The first trainable neural network — and the proof-of-concept for learning machines.", summary: "Frank Rosenblatt's single-layer neural network that could learn to classify patterns, sparking the first wave of AI optimism.", body: [
    "The perceptron, built by Frank Rosenblatt at Cornell in 1958, was the first neural network that could learn: a single layer of weighted inputs whose weights were adjusted from errors until it classified training examples correctly. Rosenblatt demonstrated it on an IBM 704 and claimed it was 'the embryo of an electronic computer that will be able to walk, talk, see, write, reproduce itself and be conscious of its existence' — the kind of overstatement that defined the era's euphoria.",
    "Marvin Minsky and Seymour Papert's 1969 book 'Perceptrons' proved that the single-layer perceptron could not learn even a simple XOR function, a result that contributed to the first AI winter. The limitation was overcome decades later with multi-layer networks and backpropagation, making the perceptron historically significant twice over: as the spark of neural AI and as the lesson that one layer is never enough."
  ], tags: ["neural networks", "history", "foundations"], related: ["neural-network", "backpropagation", "machine-learning"], sources: ["Rosenblatt, 1958 — 'The Perceptron: A Probabilistic Model for Information Storage and Organization in the Brain'"], status: "historical" },
  { id: "lenet", name: "LeNet", category: "models", year: 1998, tagline: "The convolutional network that read handwritten checks and digits.", summary: "Yann LeCun's pioneering CNN, deployed by banks for digit recognition — the first commercially successful deep network.", body: [
    "LeNet-5, designed by Yann LeCun and colleagues in 1998, introduced the architecture that would define computer vision for two decades: alternating convolutional and pooling layers that learn spatial features hierarchically, capped by fully connected layers. Trained with backpropagation, it read handwritten digits from the MNIST dataset with error rates below 1% and was deployed in the 1990s to process checks in the US banking system — making it one of the first deep neural networks in real commercial use.",
    "LeNet's ideas — convolution, weight sharing, downsampling — became the DNA of every later CNN, from AlexNet to ResNet. LeCun's early insistence that these networks could work at scale was vindicated only in 2012, when the same principles, multiplied by data and GPUs, crushed ImageNet. LeNet is remembered as proof that deep learning was commercially viable long before it was fashionable."
  ], tags: ["cnn", "vision", "history"], related: ["convolutional-neural-network", "alexnet", "computer-vision", "backpropagation"], sources: ["LeCun et al., 1998 — 'Gradient-Based Learning Applied to Document Recognition'"], status: "historical" },
  { id: "alexnet", name: "AlexNet", category: "models", year: 2012, tagline: "The network that won ImageNet by a landslide and ignited the deep-learning era.", summary: "Krizhevsky, Sutskever and Hinton's deep CNN whose 2012 ImageNet victory halved the error rate and changed the course of AI.", body: [
    "AlexNet was a convolutional neural network entered by Alex Krizhevsky, Ilya Sutskever and Geoffrey Hinton in the 2012 ImageNet Large Scale Visual Recognition Challenge. It cut the top-5 error rate from 26% to 16% — a stunning jump over every hand-crafted feature pipeline — by stacking eight learned layers, using ReLU activations, dropout and data augmentation, and training on two GPUs for days. The margin of victory was so large that the field's center of gravity shifted overnight.",
    "The ImageNet moment did for deep learning what the Dartmouth workshop did for AI: it converted skeptics, redirected funding, and ignited the GPU-driven scaling that followed. Within years, deep CNNs dominated vision, speech and games, and the same recipe — more data, more compute, deeper networks — was carried into sequence modeling, eventually culminating in transformers and the LLM era. AlexNet is the pivot point of modern AI history."
  ], tags: ["cnn", "vision", "breakthrough"], related: ["convolutional-neural-network", "deep-learning", "lenet", "computer-vision"], sources: ["Krizhevsky, Sutskever & Hinton, 2012 — 'ImageNet Classification with Deep Convolutional Neural Networks'"], status: "historical" },
  { id: "alphago", name: "AlphaGo", category: "models", year: 2016, tagline: "The system that beat a world champion at Go — a game long considered AI-proof.", summary: "DeepMind's combination of deep neural networks and Monte Carlo tree search, which defeated Lee Sedol 4–1 in 2016.", body: [
    "AlphaGo, built by DeepMind, became in March 2016 the first computer program to beat a world-class human at Go, defeating 18-time world champion Lee Sedol 4–1 in Seoul. Go's branching factor (~250 moves per position) made brute-force search impossible, so AlphaGo combined two neural networks — a policy network that proposes good moves and a value network that estimates win probability — with Monte Carlo tree search. It was trained first on human games, then on millions of self-play games.",
    "The match became a cultural event watched by tens of millions, and move 37 of game two — a stone no human professional would play, later hailed as a masterpiece — demonstrated that the machine was not merely imitating humans but discovering new strategy. AlphaGo's successors (AlphaZero, MuZero) went further by learning entirely from self-play, with no human games at all, a template that now shapes everything from protein folding (AlphaFold) to math and coding models trained with self-play-style RL."
  ], tags: ["reinforcement learning", "games", "breakthrough"], related: ["reinforcement-learning", "alphafold", "google-deepmind"], sources: ["Silver et al., 2016 — 'Mastering the Game of Go with Deep Neural Networks and Tree Search'"], status: "historical" },
  { id: "bert", name: "BERT", category: "models", year: 2018, tagline: "The bidirectional pretrained encoder that rewrote NLP — and whose ideas underpin LLMs.", summary: "Google's masked-language pretraining breakthrough that made transfer learning work for language, setting state of the art across NLP.", body: [
    "BERT (Bidirectional Encoder Representations from Transformers), released by Google in 2018, showed that a transformer pretrained on unlabeled text with a clever objective could then be fine-tuned to near-superhuman performance on a dozen NLP tasks. Its key insight was bidirectional pretraining: instead of predicting words left-to-right, it masked words and predicted them from both directions, forcing the model to build deep, context-rich representations. The paper's 11 benchmarks were all broken within days of its release.",
    "BERT made transfer learning — pretrain once, adapt everywhere — the default recipe of NLP, the same recipe GPT would take in the generative direction. Encoder models like BERT and its descendants (RoBERTa, DeBERTa) still power search ranking, classification and embedding systems at massive scale, because they are cheaper and better than decoder models at understanding. BERT is the bridge between the classifier era and the generative era of language AI."
  ], tags: ["nlp", "pretraining", "encoder"], related: ["transformer", "large-language-model", "embeddings", "gpt"], sources: ["Devlin et al., 2018 — 'BERT: Pre-training of Deep Bidirectional Transformers for Language Understanding'"], status: "historical" },
  { id: "gpt", name: "GPT Series (GPT-1 → GPT-5)", category: "models", year: 2018, tagline: "The generative pretrained transformer lineage that created the LLM era.", summary: "OpenAI's family from the 2018 research model to GPT-5, which demonstrated scaling, few-shot learning, instruction following, multimodality and reasoning.", body: [
    "The GPT lineage is the family tree of the modern LLM era. GPT-1 (2018) showed a decoder-only transformer pretrained on text could transfer to tasks; GPT-2 (2019) stunned the field with coherent generated text and was initially withheld over misuse concerns; GPT-3 (2020) revealed few-shot learning at scale — prompting by example rather than retraining — and turned 'prompt' into a verb. Each step was governed by a scaling law: model quality improved predictably with parameters, data and compute.",
    "InstructGPT (2022) added reinforcement learning from human feedback, and ChatGPT shipped that alignment to the public. GPT-4 (2023) extended the model to images and raised the ceiling on reasoning and writing, becoming the default reference point for 'frontier model'. GPT-4o (2024) added real-time voice and vision, and GPT-5 (2025) folded the o-series reasoning into a single model that thinks by default. The GPT series is also the clearest demonstration of the scaling doctrine — and the template every rival lab measured itself against, from Claude to Gemini to Llama."
  ], tags: ["openai", "generative", "frontier"], related: ["chatgpt", "large-language-model", "gpt-4", "gpt-5", "instructgpt", "openai"], sources: ["Radford et al., 2018–2019; Brown et al., 2020; OpenAI, 2023–2025 — GPT-4 & GPT-5 Technical Reports"], status: "core" },
  { id: "chatgpt", name: "ChatGPT", category: "models", year: 2022, tagline: "The product that brought AI to a billion people.", summary: "OpenAI's chat interface on GPT-3.5/4, launched November 2022 — the fastest-growing consumer app in history and the start of the generative-AI boom.", body: [
    "ChatGPT launched on November 30, 2022 as a free chat interface wrapped around OpenAI's GPT-3.5, tuned with RLHF to answer questions conversationally. It gained 100 million users in two months — the fastest consumer adoption in history — and turned 'generative AI' from a research term into a household phrase. People used it for homework, code, writing, jokes and arguments; companies rushed to integrate it; regulators scrambled to respond. The launch is the moment the AI era went mainstream.",
    "Beyond the hype, ChatGPT established the product pattern every competitor followed: an aligned chat assistant, free tier plus subscription, plugins and tool use, and a steady cadence of model upgrades (GPT-4, GPT-4o, the o-series reasoning models). It also made RLHF a household concept and opened the floodgates of AI policy debates — copyright, education, misinformation and labor. ChatGPT did not invent the LLM; it did invent the interface that made the world care."
  ], tags: ["openai", "product", "breakthrough"], related: ["gpt", "large-language-model", "reinforcement-learning-from-human-feedback", "openai"], sources: ["OpenAI, 2022 — ChatGPT launch"], status: "core" },
  { id: "dall-e", name: "DALL·E", category: "models", year: 2021, tagline: "Text-to-image generation from the team behind GPT.", summary: "OpenAI's transformer-based text-to-image models (2021–2024) that turned prompts into pictures and defined a genre.", body: [
    "DALL·E 1 (2021) showed that a scaled-up version of GPT-3's architecture, fed text-and-image tokens, could generate surprisingly coherent images from natural-language prompts — including novel combinations like 'an armchair in the shape of an avocado'. DALL·E 2 (2022) added CLIP-based conditioning and diffusion decoding to produce high-resolution, prompt-faithful images, and DALL·E 3 (2023), integrated into ChatGPT, prioritized following long, detailed prompts precisely.",
    "The DALL·E lineage turned text-to-image from a demo into a product category and pushed the entire ecosystem forward — stable diffusion's open-source explosion and Midjourney's artistic quality followed within a year. It also opened the copyright can of worms that still defines image generation: models trained on billions of web images, artists' work absorbed without consent, and an ongoing legal and ethical reckoning over what 'inspiration' means for machines."
  ], tags: ["openai", "generative", "images"], related: ["diffusion-model", "stable-diffusion", "midjourney", "generative-ai"], sources: ["Ramesh et al., 2021 — 'Zero-Shot Text-to-Image Generation'"], status: "core" },
  { id: "stable-diffusion", name: "Stable Diffusion", category: "models", year: 2022, tagline: "The open-source image model that put generation on consumer hardware.", summary: "Stability AI's latent diffusion model (2022), released open — text-to-image on a single GPU, spawning an entire ecosystem.", body: [
    "Stable Diffusion, released by Stability AI in August 2022, was the first high-quality text-to-image model the public could run locally: its latent diffusion architecture compressed images into a small latent space so that generation fit on a single consumer GPU, and the open weights meant anyone could run, fine-tune and build on it. Its arrival, weeks after DALL·E 2, democratized image generation overnight and ignited the open-source generative ecosystem.",
    "The impact was twofold: an explosion of creativity and tools — AUTOMATIC1111, ComfyUI, LoRA fine-tunes, community models on Civitai — and an equally explosive debate over consent, deepfakes and copyright, with artists confronting their styles being reproduced at scale. Later versions improved anatomy, prompt adherence and control (ControlNet, IP-Adapter), and Stable Diffusion's open-weights ethos set the template for the open-versus-closed frontier that now divides the AI industry."
  ], tags: ["generative", "images", "open source"], related: ["diffusion-model", "dall-e", "midjourney", "stability-ai"], sources: ["Rombach et al., 2022 — 'High-Resolution Image Synthesis with Latent Diffusion Models'"], status: "core" },
  { id: "midjourney", name: "Midjourney", category: "models", year: 2022, tagline: "The image model artists actually wanted to use.", summary: "A closed text-to-image service (2022) renowned for distinctive aesthetics and the strongest community in AI art.", body: [
    "Midjourney is a text-to-image model and service launched in July 2022 by an independent lab led by David Holz. Rather than chasing photorealistic fidelity like its rivals, Midjourney developed a recognizable, painterly aesthetic that designers, concept artists and game studios adopted enthusiastically, initially through Discord commands and later through a web app. Its style — cinematic lighting, strong composition, stylistic range — made 'Midjourney look' a recognizable cultural artifact.",
    "The company became a notable outlier in the industry: profitable without venture funding, tightly product-focused, and deliberately quiet about its training data. Midjourney's success demonstrated that AI art was not just a technical demo but a genuine creative medium, and it sits at the center of the copyright debates — its training corpus of billions of images without explicit consent has made it a target of artist lawsuits alongside other major generators."
  ], tags: ["generative", "images", "creative"], related: ["stable-diffusion", "dall-e", "generative-ai", "midjourney-inc"], sources: ["—"], status: "core" },
  { id: "whisper", name: "Whisper", category: "models", year: 2022, tagline: "OpenAI's speech recognition model that understands 100+ languages.", summary: "An open-weights speech-to-text model (2022) that matched or beat commercial systems and works fully offline.", body: [
    "Whisper, released by OpenAI in September 2022, is a speech recognition and translation model trained on 680,000 hours of multilingual audio — roughly a third translated to English — and built on a transformer encoder-decoder. It transcribes 100+ languages, translates to English, handles noisy audio and code-switching surprisingly well, and timestamps its output. Released with open weights under the MIT license, Whisper instantly became the default local speech engine for developers and hobbyists.",
    "Whisper's impact goes beyond quality: it made offline, private speech recognition practical on laptops and phones, powering transcription apps, meeting tools, accessibility software and local voice assistants. Variants like WhisperX add speaker diarization and word-level alignment. Its weaknesses — hallucinated text on silence and music, some accent bias — are active research topics, but Whisper demonstrated that frontier speech capability can be both open and local."
  ], tags: ["speech", "open source", "multilingual"], related: ["speech-recognition", "openai", "multimodal-ai"], sources: ["Radford et al., 2022 — 'Robust Speech Recognition via Large-Scale Weak Supervision'"], status: "core" },
  { id: "alphafold", name: "AlphaFold", category: "models", year: 2020, tagline: "The AI that solved the 50-year protein-folding problem.", summary: "DeepMind's system that predicts protein structures from amino-acid sequence, opening a new era for biology and drug discovery.", body: [
    "AlphaFold2, revealed by DeepMind in 2020, predicts the 3D structure of a protein from its amino-acid sequence with accuracy comparable to experimental methods — a problem that had defied biology for half a century. The system pairs a transformer-based architecture with a physics-inspired loss and geometry-aware attention, and its CASP14 performance stunned structural biologists. In 2021 DeepMind open-released the database of essentially every protein known to science — over 200 million structures.",
    "The impact is enormous and still unfolding: AlphaFold accelerates drug discovery, enzyme design, and understanding of disease mutations, and it became the first AI system to truly earn its keep in the physical sciences — a counterpoint to the 'AI only writes text' narrative. The 2024 Nobel Prize in Chemistry was awarded jointly to Demis Hassabis and John Jumper for AlphaFold, the first Nobel recognition of a purely AI achievement."
  ], tags: ["science", "breakthrough", "biology"], related: ["google-deepmind", "demis-hassabis", "reinforcement-learning"], sources: ["Jumper et al., 2021 — 'Highly Accurate Protein Structure Prediction with AlphaFold'"], status: "core" },
  { id: "claude", name: "Claude", category: "models", year: 2023, tagline: "Anthropic's frontier assistant, built on constitutional AI and a safety-first charter.", summary: "The Claude family (3, 3.5, 4, 4.5, Opus/Sonnet/Haiku) — known for nuance, long-context work, coding strength and Anthropic's safety posture.", body: [
    "Claude is the frontier model family from Anthropic, named by the team for Claude Shannon. It launched in March 2023 as Claude 1, and the Opus/Sonnet/Haiku tiering — flagship, workhorse, and fast small model — arrived with Claude 3 (2024). Claude's defining technical contribution is constitutional AI: models trained to critique and revise their own outputs against a written constitution rather than relying purely on human feedback, an approach aimed at making alignment scalable and legible.",
    "Claude earned a reputation for nuanced refusal behavior, long-context competence (1M-token windows for research use), careful writing and strong coding, especially through Claude Code, its agentic coding tool, and its integration with Amazon Bedrock. Anthropic's corporate identity is equally distinctive: a public-benefit corporation with a Long-Term Benefit Trust, among the earliest labs to take AI safety as its founding mission, and a leading voice in policy debates on frontier-model regulation."
  ], tags: ["anthropic", "frontier", "alignment"], related: ["anthropic", "alignment", "large-language-model", "constitutional-ai", "claude-opus-4-5"], sources: ["Anthropic, 2023–2024 — Claude model cards"], status: "core" },
  { id: "gemini", name: "Gemini", category: "models", year: 2023, tagline: "Google DeepMind's natively multimodal frontier family.", summary: "Google's Gemini line (Ultra/Pro/Flash/Nano) — born multimodal, deeply integrated with Search, Android and Workspace.", body: [
    "Gemini is Google DeepMind's frontier model family, launched December 2023 after the merger of Google Brain and DeepMind. It was built natively multimodal — trained jointly on text, images, audio and video rather than stitching separate models together — and comes in tiers from Nano (on-device) to Ultra (flagship). Gemini 1.5 introduced a 1-million-token context window, and Gemini 2.0/2.5 pushed agentic features and 'thinking' modes; Gemini 3 (late 2025) made extended reasoning a default capability.",
    "Gemini's significance is distribution: through Google Search, Android, Workspace, Chrome and the Pixel line, it reaches more users than any rival frontier model, powering AI Overviews, Project Astra's real-time assistant, and NotebookLM — whose podcast-style document summaries became a viral phenomenon. The Gemini organization also houses open models like Gemma, the AI research behind AlphaFold's Nobel, and a scale of infrastructure few labs can match."
  ], tags: ["google", "frontier", "multimodal"], related: ["google-deepmind", "multimodal-ai", "gemma", "google", "gemini-3-pro"], sources: ["Google DeepMind, 2023 — Gemini technical report"], status: "core" },
  { id: "llama", name: "Llama", category: "models", year: 2023, tagline: "Meta's open-weight family that democratized frontier-class models.", summary: "Released July 2023, Llama (1, 2, 3, 4) became the foundation of the open-weights ecosystem and the local-AI movement.", body: [
    "Llama, released by Meta in July 2023, was the first frontier-class model family released with open weights. Llama 1 and 2 offered strong performance in sizes from 7B to 70B parameters; Llama 3 (2024) scaled further with a 405B flagship, and Llama 4 (2025) adopted mixture-of-experts. The releases transformed the industry: 'Llama' is now the generic term for open-weight models, and an enormous ecosystem of fine-tunes, tools and local deployments has grown around it.",
    "Llama's open strategy was a deliberate industry move — Meta trades direct model revenue for ecosystem influence and lower-cost AI infrastructure — and it made local, private AI viable for individuals and companies alike. Llama models run on everything from phones to data centers, power countless startups, and set the standard for what 'open enough' means (weights open, training data closed). The Llama releases also forced closed labs to compete on openness, a dynamic that reshaped the entire frontier landscape."
  ], tags: ["meta", "open weights", "local ai"], related: ["meta-ai", "ollama", "quantization", "mistral"], sources: ["Touvron et al., 2023 — 'Llama: Open and Efficient Foundation Language Models'"], status: "core" },
  { id: "mistral", name: "Mistral", category: "models", year: 2023, tagline: "The Paris-based lab that proved small, efficient models can compete.", summary: "Mistral AI's open-weight models (7B, Mixtral, Large) — European AI's champion of efficiency and open licensing.", body: [
    "Mistral AI, founded in Paris in 2023 by former DeepMind and Meta researchers, became Europe's flagship AI lab by betting on efficiency: releasing small models that punched far above their weight. Its 7B model arrived months after Llama 2 with superior performance per parameter, and Mixtral 8x7B (December 2023) popularized the mixture-of-experts architecture for the mainstream. Mistral Large targeted the frontier tier, and the Le Chat assistant brought the lab's models to consumers.",
    "Mistral matters both technically and geopolitically: its models demonstrated that Europe could field credible, sovereign AI rather than depending on US hyperscalers, and its Apache-licensed releases kept the open-weights movement alive against the tide of closed frontier models. The lab's commercial model — serving open weights plus an API — became a template for 'open core' AI companies across the world."
  ], tags: ["europe", "open weights", "efficiency"], related: ["mixture-of-experts", "llama", "mistral-ai", "quantization"], sources: ["Jiang et al., 2023–2024 — Mixtral of Experts; Mistral 7B"], status: "core" },
  { id: "deepseek", name: "DeepSeek", category: "models", year: 2024, tagline: "The Chinese lab that broke the scaling-cost curve and shocked the market.", summary: "DeepSeek's open models (V3, R1) matched frontier quality at a fraction of the cost — triggering a global reassessment of AI economics.", body: [
    "DeepSeek, a Chinese AI lab spun from the quantitative trading firm High-Flyer, released DeepSeek-V3 in late 2024: a 671B-parameter mixture-of-experts model trained for under $6M of compute — a figure orders of magnitude below Western frontier training runs — with performance rivaling the best closed models. DeepSeek-R1 (January 2025) then matched OpenAI's o1-style reasoning with fully open weights and published methodology, and the announcement erased ~$1 trillion in market capitalization in a single day, becoming a global economic event.",
    "DeepSeek's significance is layered: it proved open models can reach the frontier, demonstrated that efficient MoE and reinforcement-learning-with-verifiable-rewards training can slash costs, and reshaped the geopolitics of AI — US export controls on advanced chips met an answer in algorithmic efficiency. It also supercharged the local-AI movement, since R1-class reasoning models run on consumer hardware. DeepSeek is now shorthand for the question every lab must answer: why does frontier AI have to cost this much?"
  ], tags: ["open weights", "efficiency", "reasoning"], related: ["mixture-of-experts", "llama", "o1-reasoning", "quantization"], sources: ["DeepSeek-AI, 2024–2025 — V3 & R1 technical reports"], status: "core" },
  { id: "qwen", name: "Qwen", category: "models", year: 2023, tagline: "Alibaba's open family — and the default small model of the local-AI world.", summary: "Alibaba Cloud's Qwen series (0.5B → 100B+, text, vision, audio, code, math) — dominant in open-weights benchmarks and local deployments.", body: [
    "Qwen is the open-weight model family from Alibaba Cloud, launched in 2023 and scaled through a prolific series of releases: Qwen2, Qwen2.5 (2024) and Qwen3 (2025), spanning dense and mixture-of-experts variants from 0.5B to 235B+ parameters, plus specialized models for code (Qwen2.5-Coder), math, vision (Qwen2-VL) and audio (Qwen2-Audio). The family consistently tops open-model leaderboards, and its smaller sizes run comfortably on laptops and phones.",
    "Qwen's practical significance is enormous for local AI: models like Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct and Qwen3-8B are the default 'first model' recommended by Ollama and countless local-AI guides because they are small, fast, multilingual and surprisingly capable. Alibaba's strategy mirrors Meta's — open weights as ecosystem play — and has made Qwen the workhorse of open-weights applications across Asia and the world, including the qwen3:8b model this site's companion tooling defaults to."
  ], tags: ["open weights", "local ai", "multilingual"], related: ["ollama", "llama", "alibaba-cloud", "quantization"], sources: ["Bai et al., 2023–2025 — Qwen technical reports"], status: "core" },
  { id: "phi", name: "Phi", category: "models", year: 2023, tagline: "Microsoft's 'small model, big data' experiment.", summary: "The Phi series (1→4) trained on curated synthetic data — tiny models that surprised the field with their reasoning ability.", body: [
    "Phi, a research line from Microsoft, began in 2023 with a provocative thesis: instead of training on trillions of messy web tokens, train small models on high-quality, mostly synthetic data. Phi-1 and Phi-2 were 1–3 billion parameters yet performed startlingly well on coding and reasoning benchmarks, challenging the assumption that capability requires scale. Phi-3 continued the series at 3.8B–14B, and Phi-4 pushed reasoning further.",
    "Phi's significance is the data question: it demonstrated that data quality and curation can substitute for raw scale, a finding that influences how everyone thinks about training — and that fueled the synthetic-data industry. The trade-off is breadth: small models trained on narrow, curated corpora can be weaker on general knowledge and trivia. Phi's commercial role — powering Copilot features and on-device experiences on Windows — shows the small-model market that Mistral, Qwen and Gemma all now compete in."
  ], tags: ["microsoft", "small models", "synthetic data"], related: ["microsoft-ai", "synthetic-data", "fine-tuning"], sources: ["Gunasekar et al., 2023 — 'Textbooks Are All You Need'"], status: "core" },
  { id: "gemma", name: "Gemma", category: "models", year: 2024, tagline: "Google's open-weights family for researchers and local builders.", summary: "Google DeepMind's Gemma (1, 2, 3) — the open counterpart to Gemini, engineered for edge, research and responsible use.", body: [
    "Gemma, released by Google DeepMind in February 2024, is the open-weights family that complements the closed Gemini line: 2B–27B models built from Gemini research, packaged with a focus on safety tooling, documentation and edge deployment. Gemma 2 (2024) punched well above its size with knowledge-distilled efficiency; Gemma 3 (2025) added multimodal vision and a 128k context window, with models as small as 1B optimized for phones and web.",
    "Gemma matters as evidence that Google, despite pushing Gemini commercially, is committed to the open-weights ecosystem — competing with Llama, Mistral, Qwen and Phi for the developer base that will define the next decade of applications. Its emphasis on responsible-release tooling (safety classifiers, structured evals) also reflects Google's particular sensitivity to open-model risks. For local AI, Gemma's smaller sizes make it a favorite for on-device and edge inference."
  ], tags: ["google", "open weights", "edge"], related: ["gemini", "google-deepmind", "llama", "quantization"], sources: ["Gemma Team, 2024 — 'Gemma: Open Models Based on Gemini Research and Technology'"], status: "core" },
  { id: "grok", name: "Grok", category: "models", year: 2023, tagline: "xAI's assistant with real-time X access and a rebellious brand.", summary: "Elon Musk's xAI model family — real-time data from X, fast Grok 3, and one of the largest training clusters on Earth.", body: [
    "Grok is the AI assistant from xAI, Elon Musk's company founded in 2023, launched in November 2023 with a distinctive pitch: unfiltered tone and real-time knowledge streamed from the X (Twitter) platform. Grok 2 and Grok 3 (2025) advanced the frontier, with Grok 3 claiming top benchmark positions at release and introducing the fastest thinking-mode tiers; the line continued through Grok 4 and 4.1 (2025), which made deep reasoning and native image generation its defaults. The brand is deliberately irreverent, and xAI frames Grok as the 'maximally truth-seeking' alternative to 'woke' guardrails.",
    "Grok's strategic significance is scale and data: xAI built Colossus, one of the world's largest AI training clusters (100k+ GPUs, built in months), and its integration with X gives it a live feed of human discourse unmatched by other labs — a double-edged asset given X's content moderation controversies. Grok's release also made 'real-time training data' a competitive battleground, and xAI's merger with X consolidated data, distribution and compute under one roof."
  ], tags: ["xai", "real-time", "frontier"], related: ["xai", "large-language-model", "twitter-x", "grok-4-1"], sources: ["xAI, 2023–2025 — Grok model cards"], status: "core" },
  { id: "o1-reasoning", name: "o1 & Reasoning Models", category: "models", year: 2024, tagline: "Models that think before they answer.", summary: "OpenAI's o1, o3 and the 2025 'thinking' wave (DeepSeek R1, Gemini 2.5, Claude 3.7/4) — long chain-of-thought reasoning trained with RL.", body: [
    "OpenAI's o1 (September 2024) introduced the reasoning-model paradigm: before answering, the model generates hidden chains of thought, evaluating and refining its own reasoning — an internal 'thinking' phase trained with reinforcement learning against verifiable rewards rather than human preference alone. o1 stunned mathematicians and programmers, and o3 (2025) pushed further toward frontier-level science and code benchmarks, with ARC-AGI scores that reignited debates about general intelligence; o4 (2025) then trained tool use directly into the reasoning loop, and the line ended as GPT-5 absorbed the o-series.",
    "The paradigm swept the industry within months: DeepSeek-R1 replicated it with open weights, Gemini 2.5 Flash Thinking and Claude's extended thinking offered their own versions, and 'thinking mode' became a standard product toggle. Reasoning models changed the economics of capability — spending more compute at inference time buys better answers, blurring the line between training and thinking — and they sharpened the alignment question, since hidden reasoning can neither be inspected nor trusted automatically."
  ], tags: ["reasoning", "frontier", "reinforcement learning"], related: ["deepseek", "chain-of-thought", "openai", "reinforcement-learning", "o4"], sources: ["OpenAI, 2024 — 'Learning to Reason with LLMs'"], status: "core" },
  { id: "sora", name: "Sora & Video Generation", category: "models", year: 2024, tagline: "Text-to-video models that brought motion to generative AI.", summary: "OpenAI's Sora and rivals (Veo, Runway, Kling) — generating minutes of coherent video from text, with physics learned rather than simulated.", body: [
    "Sora, previewed by OpenAI in February 2024, generates up to a minute of coherent, high-fidelity video from a text prompt, maintaining object persistence, camera motion and approximate physics across shots. Built on diffusion-transformer hybrids trained on massive video corpora, it learned visual dynamics the way language models learn grammar — not by simulating physics but by predicting pixels. Rivals matched quickly: Google's Veo, Runway's Gen models, Kuaishou's Kling and Meta's Movie Gen.",
    "Video generation is the fastest-advancing generative frontier and the most contentious: it promises to collapse the cost of filmmaking and creative production, while enabling deepfake video at scale — a leap in the realism of synthetic misinformation that has pushed watermarking (C2PA provenance) and detection to the policy agenda. The models also signal the endgame of generative AI: unified systems that produce any modality from text, with video the hardest and most consequential of all."
  ], tags: ["generative", "video", "frontier"], related: ["diffusion-model", "generative-ai", "deepfake", "multimodal-ai", "veo"], sources: ["OpenAI, 2024 — 'Video generation models as world simulators'"], status: "emerging" },
  { id: "copilot", name: "GitHub Copilot", category: "models", year: 2021, tagline: "The AI pair programmer that launched the coding-assistant era.", summary: "GitHub and OpenAI's code completion tool (2021) — now an agentic platform with Copilot Workspace and Codex — the first mass-market AI dev tool.", body: [
    "GitHub Copilot, launched in June 2021 as a technical preview, was the first mainstream AI coding tool: a fine-tuned Codex model that completes code inline as you type, trained on public repositories. Its launch turned 'AI pair programmer' from slogan into product and defined the category that Cursor, Codeium and every IDE vendor subsequently chased. Copilot evolved from autocomplete to chat, to multi-file editing with Copilot Edits, to agentic workflows with Copilot Workspace and OpenAI Codex, and now ships inside VS Code, JetBrains IDEs and GitHub itself.",
    "Copilot's significance is broader than convenience: it changed developer expectations, accelerated the productivity debate (studies found meaningful speedups for experienced developers, smaller gains for novices), and raised hard questions about licensing and compensation — GitHub's Copilot training on public code triggered the class action against the model providers and the ongoing copyright wars. It also proved the business model: dev tools were where AI would pay for itself first, and every frontier lab now ships a coding agent."
  ], tags: ["coding", "product", "microsoft"], related: ["github-copilot", "microsoft-ai", "agents", "codex"], sources: ["GitHub, 2021 — 'Introducing GitHub Copilot'"], status: "core" },
  { id: "resnet", name: "ResNet", category: "models", year: 2015, tagline: "The skip-connection network that made very deep learning possible.", summary: "Microsoft's residual network (2015) whose identity skip connections let networks reach hundreds of layers — the architecture behind almost every modern deep network.", body: [
    "ResNet, introduced by Kaiming He and colleagues at Microsoft Research in 2015, solved the paradox that deeper networks were harder to train than shallower ones. By adding skip connections that let each block learn a residual — the change to an identity mapping rather than the mapping itself — gradients could flow almost unhindered through hundreds of layers. ResNet won the 2015 ImageNet competition with a 152-layer network and became the default backbone for vision for years.",
    "The residual connection is now a structural assumption across deep learning: transformers use them, diffusion models use them, and virtually every modern architecture stacks residual blocks. ResNet's deeper lesson — that well-designed shortcuts in optimization can unlock scale — is one of the most widely copied ideas in the field, and its descendants (ResNeXt, EfficientNet) carried vision to the transformer era. It is the rare paper whose name became a verb: 'we resnet-ified the architecture'."
  ], tags: ["vision", "architecture", "breakthrough"], related: ["alexnet", "computer-vision", "deep-learning", "lenet"], sources: ["He et al., 2016 — 'Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition'"], status: "historical" },
  { id: "dqn", name: "Deep Q-Network (DQN)", category: "models", year: 2015, tagline: "The system that mastered Atari from raw pixels — deep RL's first triumph.", summary: "DeepMind's 2015 agent that learned 49 Atari games directly from screen pixels using deep Q-learning, inaugurating the deep-reinforcement-learning era.", body: [
    "Deep Q-Network, published by Mnih et al. at DeepMind in 2015, combined Q-learning with a deep convolutional network that took raw game pixels as input and output action values. The trick that made it work — experience replay and a target network to stabilize learning — let the agent surpass human performance on dozens of Atari 2600 games, learning each one from scratch through trial and error. It was the first convincing demonstration that a single algorithm could master many tasks from raw sensory input.",
    "DQN opened the deep-RL era that produced AlphaGo, Dota 2 and StarCraft champions, and its successors (Double DQN, Dueling DQN, Rainbow) refined the recipe. Its importance is also cautionary: DQN was brittle and sample-hungry, and the Atari benchmarks it conquered proved easy to game. The line from DQN to modern reasoning models runs through the same core idea — learning from rewards rather than labels — which is now being applied to language models themselves."
  ], tags: ["reinforcement learning", "games", "deepmind"], related: ["reinforcement-learning", "alphago", "google-deepmind", "rich-sutton"], sources: ["Mnih et al., 2015 — 'Human-level control through deep reinforcement learning'"], status: "historical" },
  { id: "vae", name: "Variational Autoencoder (VAE)", category: "models", year: 2013, tagline: "The generative model that gave AI a principled way to imagine.", summary: "Kingma & Welling's 2013 latent-variable model — the probabilistic framework behind modern generative AI and the ancestor of latent diffusion.", body: [
    "The variational autoencoder, introduced by Diederik Kingma and Max Welling in 2013, learns to compress data into a structured latent space and then generate new data by sampling from it. Its key contribution was variational inference made practical: by maximizing an evidence lower bound, the network learns a smooth, continuous latent representation where nearby points decode to similar outputs. It was the first deep generative model with a rigorous probabilistic foundation.",
    "The VAE's legacy is everywhere in modern AI: Stable Diffusion literally runs its diffusion process in a VAE-learned latent space, and the encoder-decoder structure of modern generative systems descends from it. Variants like VQ-VAE (vector-quantized, used in DALL-E 1 and audio models) fixed its one weakness — blurry samples from the Gaussian prior — while its theoretical rigor made it the standard tool for representation learning and anomaly detection. Generative AI's ability to 'imagine' begins with this paper."
  ], tags: ["generative", "latent space", "theory"], related: ["generative-ai", "stable-diffusion", "dall-e", "embeddings"], sources: ["Kingma & Welling, 2013 — 'Auto-Encoding Variational Bayes'"], status: "historical" },
  { id: "alphazero", name: "AlphaZero / MuZero", category: "models", year: 2017, tagline: "The system that mastered chess, Go and shogi with no human knowledge at all.", summary: "DeepMind's 2017 successor to AlphaGo — self-play reinforcement learning with zero human games, which became the world's strongest chess player in hours.", body: [
    "AlphaZero, published by DeepMind in 2017, generalized AlphaGo into a single algorithm that masters any perfect-information game through pure self-play: it plays millions of games against itself, learns a policy and value function from scratch, and searches with Monte Carlo tree search — no human games, no hand-coded heuristics. After just four hours of training it beat the world-champion chess program Stockfish, and later versions dominated Go and shogi. MuZero extended the idea by learning the game's rules themselves from raw observations.",
    "The AlphaZero line is the clearest demonstration of the 'bitter lesson': general learning algorithms with massive self-play compute exceed the best human-engineered knowledge. Its significance now reaches far beyond games — the same self-play + RL recipe is being applied to mathematics, coding and reasoning models (AlphaProof, and the RL-trained 'thinking' models), where the environment is verifiable answers rather than a board. The game AI that learns its own rules is the template for the agentic era."
  ], tags: ["reinforcement learning", "self-play", "deepmind"], related: ["alphago", "reinforcement-learning", "google-deepmind", "o1-reasoning"], sources: ["Silver et al., 2018 — 'A general reinforcement learning algorithm that masters chess, shogi, and Go through self-play'; Schrittwieser et al., 2020 — MuZero"], status: "historical" },
  { id: "t5", name: "T5 (Text-to-Text Transfer Transformer)", category: "models", year: 2019, tagline: "The 'everything is text' model that unified NLP into one framework.", summary: "Google's 2019 encoder-decoder transformer that recast every NLP task as text-to-text — the basis of the massive FLAN instruction-tuning line that powered the LLM era.", body: [
    "T5, introduced by Google in 2019, made a radical simplifying bet: treat every language task — translation, summarization, question answering, classification — as text-to-text, feeding text in and generating text out. Trained on the Colossal Clean Crawled Corpus with a span-corruption objective, T5 set state of the art across dozens of benchmarks and provided the cleanest demonstration yet that a single pretrained transformer could be adapted to everything. Its successors (mT5 for multilingual, FLAN-T5) became industry workhorses.",
    "T5's legacy is the instruction-tuning paradigm: FLAN (Fine-tuned Language Net), built from T5, showed that training on many tasks phrased as instructions made models follow new instructions they had never seen — the exact capability that ChatGPT later productized. Every modern instruction-following model descends from this line, and T5's 'one framework for all text' philosophy quietly settled the architecture debates of its era: the answer was text in, text out, scale."
  ], tags: ["nlp", "pretraining", "instruction tuning"], related: ["transformer", "bert", "gpt", "large-language-model"], sources: ["Raffel et al., 2020 — 'Exploring the Limits of Transfer Learning with a Unified Text-to-Text Transformer'"], status: "historical" },
  { id: "gpt-4o", name: "GPT-4o", category: "models", year: 2024, tagline: "OpenAI's real-time multimodal model — voice, vision and text in one.", summary: "The 2024 omni-model that made voice conversation with AI feel natural, with real-time audio, image and video understanding — the interface era's defining release.", body: [
    "GPT-4o ('omni'), released by OpenAI in May 2024, unified text, audio and vision in a single natively multimodal model, processing them together rather than stitching separate speech and vision systems. Its breakthrough was latency and expressiveness: the model responds to spoken voice in as little as a few hundred milliseconds, detects emotion in tone, and can see your screen — turning the assistant from a chat box into a live conversational presence. The voice demo, with its warm real-time banter, became one of the most viewed product moments in AI history.",
    "GPT-4o reset the product bar for the whole industry — Google's Gemini Live, Anthropic's real-time audio and every voice assistant followed within months — and its 'omni' architecture (a single model, not a pipeline) became the template for frontier multimodal design. Its release also escalated the safety stakes: real-time voice means real-time manipulation risk, and its uncanny naturalness revived debates about anthropomorphism, AI companionship and the ethics of synthetic voices."
  ], tags: ["openai", "multimodal", "voice"], related: ["gpt", "chatgpt", "multimodal-ai", "openai"], sources: ["OpenAI, 2024 — 'Hello GPT-4o' and model card"], status: "core" },
  { id: "wavenet", name: "WaveNet", category: "models", year: 2016, tagline: "The neural network that made machines speak like humans.", summary: "DeepMind's 2016 autoregressive audio model that produced eerily natural speech and music — the deep-learning breakthrough behind modern text-to-speech.", body: [
    "WaveNet, published by DeepMind in 2016, generated raw audio waveforms one sample at a time using dilated causal convolutions, producing speech that human listeners rated more natural than any existing system — and passable piano music. It proved that neural networks could model the raw, continuous structure of sound rather than the compressed intermediate representations used by earlier systems, and it directly powered Google Assistant's voices and the Google TTS cloud service.",
    "WaveNet's significance is twofold: it was a landmark of autoregressive generation — predicting the next sample, exactly like an LLM predicts the next token — and it opened the generative-audio era now populated by ElevenLabs, Suno and voice cloning. The WaveNet trick of causal dilated convolutions later migrated into text-to-speech vocoders (WaveGlow, HiFi-GAN) and even sequence modeling research. Every time an AI voice sounds unsettlingly human, you are hearing WaveNet's descendants."
  ], tags: ["speech", "generative", "audio"], related: ["speech-recognition", "generative-ai", "whisper"], sources: ["van den Oord et al., 2016 — 'WaveNet: A Generative Model for Raw Audio'"], status: "historical" },
  { id: "clip", name: "CLIP", category: "models", year: 2021, tagline: "The model that taught machines to connect images to words.", summary: "OpenAI's 2021 vision-language model trained on 400M image-text pairs — the zero-shot backbone behind DALL-E 2, Stable Diffusion conditioning and the multimodal era.", body: [
    "CLIP (Contrastive Language-Image Pre-training), released by OpenAI in 2021, learns a shared embedding space where images and their text descriptions map near each other, trained on 400 million image-text pairs scraped from the web. The result is zero-shot vision: CLIP can classify images into arbitrary text-defined categories it never saw during training, and it made text-conditioned image generation possible by providing a semantic bridge between prompts and pixels.",
    "CLIP is the quiet backbone of the generative-image era — Stable Diffusion's text encoder and DALL-E 2's prior both rely on CLIP embeddings — and it opened the broader vision-language research wave (BLIP, SigLIP, and the multimodal encoders inside GPT-4V, Gemini and Claude). Its pathologies are equally influential: CLIP's web training data baked in stereotypes, and its patchy failure modes became the canonical examples in the AI-bias literature. Few models have done more to connect the two halves of AI — language and vision."
  ], tags: ["multimodal", "vision-language", "embeddings"], related: ["multimodal-ai", "embeddings", "stable-diffusion", "dall-e"], sources: ["Radford et al., 2021 — 'Learning Transferable Visual Models From Natural Language Supervision'"], status: "core" },
  { id: "gpt-4", name: "GPT-4", category: "models", year: 2023, tagline: "The multimodal frontier model that defined 2023 — and the benchmark bar every rival chased.", summary: "OpenAI's 2023 flagship: passed the bar exam, scored 86% on MMLU, added image understanding — the release that made 'frontier model' a household term.", body: [
    "GPT-4, released by OpenAI in March 2023, was the model that set the frontier's reference point: it passed the uniform bar exam in the top 10% of human test-takers, scored 86% on MMLU (versus GPT-3.5's 70%), and added image understanding alongside text. Trained at unprecedented scale, its release came with a careful safety review — the first time a major lab staged a public alignment-and-evals campaign around a frontier deployment. Within months 'GPT-4' had become the industry's default benchmark baseline and the model enterprise buyers meant when they said 'AI'.",
    "GPT-4's legacy is structural as much as technical: it established the pattern of evaluating models against professional and academic human performance, popularized system prompts and steerable assistants, and seeded the applications — coding agents, document analysis, tutoring — that defined the boom. Its successors each extended one of GPT-4's axes: GPT-4o's real-time multimodality, the o-series reasoning models, and GPT-5's unified thinking. But GPT-4 remains the release against which the modern frontier era is measured."
  ], tags: ["openai", "frontier", "multimodal"], related: ["openai", "gpt", "gpt-4o", "large-language-model", "multimodal-ai"], sources: ["OpenAI, 2023 — 'GPT-4 Technical Report'"], status: "core" },
  { id: "gpt-5", name: "GPT-5", category: "models", year: 2025, tagline: "The 2025 flagship that made reasoning the default mode of the assistant era.", summary: "OpenAI's unified 2025 frontier model — long-horizon reasoning, agentic tool use and multimodal input in a single system — the current reference point of the GPT lineage.", body: [
    "GPT-5, launched by OpenAI in 2025, unified the company's two model lines — the conversational GPT series and the o-series reasoning models — into a single system that reasons for as long as a task demands. Agentic patterns became defaults rather than modes: persistent tool use, code execution, long-horizon planning and deep web research were built in, its benchmark runs pushed past prior frontiers on coding, science and instruction following, and its deployment was paired with OpenAI's strongest evals-and-safety review to date.",
    "GPT-5 matters beyond the numbers: it marked the moment reasoning stopped being a toggle and became the default behavior of frontier assistants, and its release reset the competitive bar that Claude, Gemini and Grok measure themselves against. Its rollout also sharpened the era's defining debates — the cost and energy of frontier inference, the opacity of internal reasoning, and whether capability gains are still tracking a responsible deployment pace."
  ], tags: ["openai", "frontier", "reasoning"], related: ["gpt-4", "openai", "o1-reasoning", "agents", "ai-safety"], sources: ["OpenAI, 2025 — GPT-5 system card"], status: "core" },
  { id: "kimi", name: "Kimi (K2)", category: "models", year: 2024, tagline: "Moonshot AI's long-context open model — the agentic-workhorse surprise of 2025.", summary: "The Kimi family from Beijing's Moonshot AI: trillion-parameter MoE models with million-token context and strong agentic coding, released open in 2025.", body: [
    "Kimi, developed by Moonshot AI in Beijing, first made its name in 2023–2024 with long-context chat: the Kimi consumer app let users drop entire documents and books into a 128k-to-1M-token window. The model line scaled dramatically with K2 (2025), a trillion-parameter mixture-of-experts model released with open weights that matched closed frontier models on agentic coding and long-horizon tasks — 'the sleeper hit of the open-model year', in many evals. K2's open release made it a favorite for autonomous coding agents and local deployment.",
    "Kimi's significance is the proof it offers that frontier capability is no longer a US monopoly: Moonshot trained K2 at a fraction of Western budgets and open-sourced it, joining DeepSeek, Qwen and GLM in the Chinese open-model wave. Its long-context strength also pushed the industry's context wars — million-token windows went from demo to default across the frontier within a year of Kimi's push."
  ], tags: ["china", "open weights", "long context"], related: ["moonshot-ai", "qwen", "deepseek", "glm", "context-window"], sources: ["Moonshot AI, 2024–2025 — Kimi K2 technical report"], status: "core" },
  { id: "glm", name: "GLM", category: "models", year: 2023, tagline: "Zhipu's open transformer family — the academic-rooted challenger from Tsinghua.", summary: "The GLM series (GLM-4, GLM-4.5) from Zhipu AI: capable open-weights models for text, vision and coding, among China's strongest frontier families.", body: [
    "GLM (General Language Model) is the model family from Zhipu AI, a company spun out of Tsinghua University's KEG lab in 2019. GLM-4 (2024) and GLM-4.5 (2025) delivered frontier-adjacent performance with open weights, strong Chinese-English bilingual ability, and competitive coding and agentic benchmarks — the GLM-4.5 series introduced a mixture-of-experts flagship and a lightweight 'Air' model aimed at edge devices. The family also powers Zhipu's consumer assistant ChatGLM, one of China's most-used AI products.",
    "GLM matters as part of the Chinese open-model ecosystem that reshaped global AI economics: alongside DeepSeek, Qwen and Kimi, Zhipu showed that open-weights models could reach within striking distance of the closed frontier. Its academic lineage — born from a university research group — also makes it a living argument for the value of public-sector AI research, even as geopolitical scrutiny of Chinese model releases intensifies."
  ], tags: ["china", "open weights", "academic"], related: ["zhipu-ai", "kimi", "qwen", "mixture-of-experts", "deepseek"], sources: ["Zhipu AI, 2024–2025 — GLM-4 / GLM-4.5 technical reports"], status: "core" },
  { id: "veo", name: "Veo", category: "models", year: 2024, tagline: "Google's cinematic text-to-video model — the polished rival to Sora.", summary: "Google DeepMind's Veo (and Veo 2, 2024) — high-fidelity video generation with real physics, camera control and native audio, integrated across YouTube and Gemini.", body: [
    "Veo, announced by Google DeepMind in 2024, is Google's answer to Sora: text-to-video generation at cinematic quality, with coherent motion, real-world physics and — in Veo 2 — native audio and precise camera controls. Built on diffusion-transformer hybrids and trained at unprecedented video scale, Veo 2's clips were widely judged the most realistic of the major text-to-video systems, with studios and directors adopting it for previsualization and production work.",
    "Veo's significance is distribution and safety-by-default: integrated into Google's ecosystem — YouTube Shorts, the Gemini app, Flow — it put professional-grade video generation in front of hundreds of millions of users, while Google's SynthID watermarking (invisible marks embedded in every frame) made Veo the strongest mainstream example of provenance-by-design against deepfakes. The Veo–Sora rivalry became the public face of the video-generation race, each release resetting what 'prompt to film' means."
  ], tags: ["google", "video", "generative"], related: ["sora", "google-deepmind", "multimodal-ai", "diffusion-model", "generative-ai"], sources: ["Google DeepMind, 2024–2025 — Veo / Veo 2 announcements"], status: "emerging" },
  { id: "claude-opus-4-5", name: "Claude Opus 4.5", category: "models", year: 2025, tagline: "Anthropic's 2025 flagship — the agentic coding workhorse of the frontier.", summary: "The Opus 4.5 release (2025): Anthropic's most capable model — records on coding and long-horizon agent tasks, and the engine behind Claude Code's leap from demo to production.", body: [
    "Claude Opus 4.5, launched by Anthropic in late 2025, is the top tier of the Claude 4 generation and the model the lab's agentic bets run on. It set new highs on coding benchmarks (SWE-bench Verified, LiveCodeBench), long-horizon computer-use tasks and nuanced reasoning, and it underpins Claude Code's agentic workflows — multi-file edits, test-driven fixing and long autonomous sessions — that made Anthropic's coding agent the enterprise default of 2026. Anthropic paired the release with extended thinking budgets and its strictest Responsible Scaling Policy review to date.",
    "Opus 4.5's significance is the production agent: where earlier models demoed agentic capability, 4.5 was deployed as infrastructure — inside enterprises, IDEs and the Claude API — with usage-based pricing that made long agentic sessions economically viable. Its rivalry with GPT-5, Gemini 3 Pro and o4 defined the 2025–26 frontier, each release trading benchmark crowns, and its safety-first framing kept the alignment question at the center of the race."
  ], tags: ["anthropic", "frontier", "agents"], related: ["claude", "anthropic", "o4", "gpt-5", "agents", "ai-safety"], sources: ["Anthropic, 2025 — Claude Opus 4.5 system card"], status: "core" },
  { id: "gemini-3-pro", name: "Gemini 3 Pro", category: "models", year: 2025, tagline: "Google DeepMind's 2025 flagship — reasoning and multimodality fused into one agent-native system.", summary: "Gemini 3 Pro (late 2025): Google's most capable model — million-token context, native audio and video understanding, and extended thinking by default, powering the agentic layer of Android, Search and Workspace.", body: [
    "Gemini 3 Pro, released by Google DeepMind in late 2025 after an extended public testing phase, is the flagship of the Gemini 3 generation: natively multimodal (text, images, audio and video in a single model), with a million-token context window and extended reasoning as a default rather than a mode. It anchors Google's agentic push — computer-use tasks, browser automation and grounded research through Search and NotebookLM — and its integration across Android, Workspace and the Gemini app gave it the widest distribution of any frontier model.",
    "Gemini 3 Pro's significance is Google's return to the top of the frontier: months of benchmark records on AIME, GPQA and MMMU, and a product cadence that forced rivals to answer. Its release also sharpened the platform question — whether distribution (Google's) or model capability (OpenAI's and Anthropic's) wins the agent era — and its DeepMind origin kept it the most research-credentialed of the 2025 flagships."
  ], tags: ["google", "frontier", "agents"], related: ["gemini", "google-deepmind", "multimodal-ai", "gpt-5", "claude-opus-4-5"], sources: ["Google DeepMind, 2025 — Gemini 3 Pro technical report"], status: "core" },
  { id: "o4", name: "o4", category: "models", year: 2025, tagline: "OpenAI's agent-native reasoning model — thinking and tool use in one loop.", summary: "o4 (2025), the successor to o3: OpenAI's reasoning model with tool use, vision and long-horizon planning trained into the thinking loop — the last standalone model before reasoning folded into GPT-5.", body: [
    "o4, released by OpenAI in 2025, is the o-series' step past o3 — and its final act as a separate product line. Its defining advance was training tool use directly into the reasoning loop: the model browses, runs code and calls APIs mid-thought, treating tools as part of thinking rather than an afterthought. On coding, competitive programming and agentic benchmarks it set new marks, and its 'think, then act, then verify' loop became the template for agent-native architectures across the industry.",
    "o4 matters historically as the hinge between the reasoning era and the agent era: OpenAI shipped it as the standalone peak of the o-series, then folded the entire line into GPT-5, which reasons, plans and uses tools as one system. Its release also pushed the debates around test-time compute and hidden chain-of-thought to their current form — more thinking, more tools, and the same questions about auditability and cost."
  ], tags: ["openai", "reasoning", "agents"], related: ["o1-reasoning", "gpt-5", "openai", "agents", "test-time-compute"], sources: ["OpenAI, 2025 — o4 system card"], status: "core" },
  { id: "grok-4-1", name: "Grok 4.1", category: "models", year: 2025, tagline: "xAI's 2025 frontier — deep reasoning, real-time X and image generation at speed.", summary: "Grok 4.1 (2025): xAI's most capable model — thinking mode at scale, real-time X data and native image generation (Aurora) — trained on the expanded Colossus cluster.", body: [
    "Grok 4.1, released by xAI in 2025, is the refinement of the Grok 4 generation: frontier-level reasoning with dramatically faster thinking modes, real-time knowledge streamed from X, and native image generation via its Aurora model. Trained on Colossus — expanded into one of the largest compute clusters ever built — it posted top-of-the-board runs on math, science and coding benchmarks within months of release, and its deep-search agentic research mode made it a favorite among the X-native audience.",
    "Grok 4.1's significance is the consolidation of Musk's AI empire: after the X merger, the model family gained data, distribution and compute under one roof, and 4.1's rapid cadence showed xAI could match the frontier's release tempo. Its 'maximally truth-seeking' branding and lighter guardrails kept it the most controversial of the 2025 flagships — loved and criticized for exactly the same properties — while its speed-to-capability ratio reset expectations for what one company can do with enough GPUs."
  ], tags: ["xai", "frontier", "reasoning"], related: ["grok", "xai", "o4", "gpt-5", "large-language-model"], sources: ["xAI, 2025 — Grok 4.1 model card"], status: "core" }
];
